  ('HIS-03', 'his-03-pacific-tattoo-knowledge-systems-continuity-genealogy-revival', 'history', 23, 'Pacific Tattoo Knowledge Systems', 'Pacific Tattoo Knowledge Systems: Continuity, Genealogy, Service, Rank, Gender, and Revival', 'Sāmoan tatau, Māori tā moko, and Marquesan marks as distinct systems of genealogy, rank, and revival.', 'In the Pacific, a tattoo is a genealogy you wear. It is given by authority, not bought.', '["Pacific","Tatau","Tā moko","Revival"]', 'HIS-03_pacific-tattoo-knowledge-systems-continuity-genealogy-revival.md', 1417, 7, '2026-07-20', '# HIS-03 — Pacific Tattoo Knowledge Systems: Continuity, Genealogy, Service, Rank, Gender, and Revival

## 1. Abstract and Scope

This paper treats Sāmoan tatau, Māori tā moko, Marquesan patutiki, and the wider Pacific tattoo tradition as distinct knowledge systems rather than variations on a single "Polynesian tattoo" theme, centering Indigenous scholarship, language, authority structures, colonial disruption, and contemporary renewal as the organizing framework, per the corpus plan''s explicit instruction. This paper is substantially a deepening and anthropological reframing of material already established at length in STY-04 (Living Lineages), which this paper cross-references extensively rather than duplicates; its original contribution is treating these three (and related) traditions specifically as *knowledge systems* — bodies of genealogical, cosmological, and social information encoded in and transmitted through tattoo practice — rather than primarily as visual/decorative styles, a framing shift with direct consequences for how the database should model these entries'' cultural-ownership fields.

## 2. Definitions and Boundary Criteria

**Knowledge system (as this paper''s organizing concept).** A tattoo tradition understood not merely as a decorative style but as an integrated system for transmitting and encoding genealogy (whakapapa), rank, service obligation, gender role, and cosmological understanding — knowledge that is, in the traditions surveyed here, inseparable from *who* may apply it, *how* it is authorized, and *what* authority governs its correct execution. This paper treats Sāmoan tatau, Māori tā moko, and Marquesan patutiki as three distinct knowledge systems (per STY-04''s own definitional boundary), while acknowledging shared broader Pacific/Polynesian continuities (STY-04''s polynesian entry) without collapsing the three into an undifferentiated whole.

## 3. Chronology, Geography, and Transmission

This paper defers to STY-04 (Section 3) for the detailed chronology of Māori tā moko (pre-contact through toi moko-era colonial disruption to 1970s–80s revival), Sāmoan tatau (documented unbroken continuity, Mallon and Galliot''s 2018 academic history), and Marquesan patutiki (near-total suppression and catastrophic depopulation, 1980s–90s-onward Marquesan-led revival, the 2019 film *Patutiki*). This paper''s specific contribution is foregrounding the *knowledge-system* framing within that already-established chronology: Sāmoan tatau''s documented continuity is significant specifically because it represents unbroken transmission of a complete knowledge system (motif meaning, application protocol, hereditary practitioner authority, and social function) rather than merely unbroken visual-style continuity; conversely, Marquesan patutiki''s near-total suppression represented a near-total loss of an integrated knowledge system, not merely a decorative tradition, which is why its revival — as documented in STY-04 — has required Marquesan practitioners to actively reconstruct not just visual motifs but their meanings and application protocols from fragmentary surviving knowledge and colonial-era ethnographic documentation, a qualitatively harder task than reviving a visual style alone.

## 4. Per-Entry Descriptions

This paper maps to the same catalogIds as STY-04 (tribal, maori, polynesian, samoan, marquesan) but reframes them specifically through the knowledge-system lens: maori''s tā moko as a whakapapa-encoding system requiring tohunga-tā-moko authority to correctly "read" and apply; samoan''s tatau as a service-and-rank-encoding system requiring tufuga tā tatau hereditary authority; marquesan''s patutiki as a cosmological/ancestral-memory-encoding system currently being actively reconstructed by community-led revival; and polynesian as the broader family within which these distinct systems, and others (Tongan, Hawaiian, and others not treated at the same depth in this corpus), are related but not interchangeable.

## 5. Materials, Tools, and Making Process

This paper defers entirely to STY-04 (Section 5) and TEC-06 (Section 5) for tools (uhi, au, hand-tapped combs) and technical process, adding only the observation that tool and technique in these traditions are themselves part of the knowledge system rather than separable from it — the specific comb, mallet, and rhythm of hand-tapped application in Sāmoan tatau, for instance, is documented (STY-04, Section 8) as integral to the pain-endurance and service meaning the finished tatau carries, meaning technique cannot be extracted from the tradition''s meaning-system without altering what the finished tattoo signifies.

## 6. Visual Grammar

This paper defers to STY-04 (Section 6) for the specific visual grammar of each tradition (Māori koru/manaia/moko kauae; Sāmoan pe''a/malu compositional structure; Marquesan patutiki density and figurative motifs), adding the observation that visual grammar in these systems is not freely recombinable decorative vocabulary but is governed by the same knowledge-system authority (tohunga, tufuga) that governs application — a specific arrangement or motif combination may correctly or incorrectly represent a specific genealogy or rank, a constraint absent from purely decorative tattoo styles documented elsewhere in this corpus.

## 7. Skin, Anatomy, Placement, Healing, Aging, and Technical Limitations

This paper defers to STY-04 (Section 7) for placement, pain, and healing documentation (facial moko/moko kauae, full pe''a/malu coverage, ten-to-fifteen-session physical demands), noting that in a knowledge-system framing, placement itself often carries genealogical/status information (which body region receives which pattern) rather than being a purely aesthetic or practical choice — a distinction this paper argues the database''s placement-suitability fields should reflect for these three entries specifically, differently from how placement suitability is modeled for purely decorative styles.

## 8. Social Meaning, Identity, Ritual, Memory, and Client Narrative

This paper defers to STY-04 (Section 8) for the detailed social-meaning documentation already established (whakapapa and mana for Māori; covenant and tautua/service for Sāmoan; ancestral memory and worldview for Marquesan), treating that material as this paper''s core substantive content rather than restating it, consistent with this corpus''s general practice of cross-referencing rather than duplicating prior papers'' authoritative treatment.

## 9. Cultural Ownership, Sacred/Restricted Elements, and Ethical Use

**This section is this paper''s primary original contribution, reframing STY-04''s cultural-ownership findings (Section 9) through the specific lens of knowledge-system authority rather than generic "sacred/restricted" flagging.** Because tā moko, tatau, and patutiki each encode genealogical, service, and cosmological information under specific community-recognized authority structures (tohunga-tā-moko, tufuga tā tatau, and Marquesan community-led revival authority respectively), an AI system generating designs invoking these traditions is not merely risking generic cultural insensitivity but risking the generation of *incorrect or fabricated genealogical/status claims* dressed in authentic-looking visual form — a distinct and, this paper argues, more serious harm than decorative misappropriation alone, since a design falsely implying specific rank, service history, or genealogical descent within these knowledge systems could actively mislead both the wearer and observers within communities where these systems remain living and socially meaningful. This reframing supports and sharpens, rather than contradicts, STY-04''s existing recommendation that the database steer non-community AI-generated requests toward kirituhi-style Māori/Sāmoan-influenced ornament (which makes no genealogical/status claim) rather than purported authentic tā moko or tatau.

## 10. Disputes, Regional Variation, Source Limitations, and Confidence Level

**No new disputes were identified beyond those already documented at length in STY-04.** This paper''s primary methodological note is that the knowledge-system framing offered here is this paper''s own analytical contribution — a way of organizing and foregrounding STY-04''s existing findings for the specific purposes of the corpus''s history/anthropology module — rather than a claim sourced to any single new citation. Confidence: high, since this framing is a direct, conservative extension of STY-04''s own extensively sourced findings (Mallon and Galliot 2018; Krutak; multiple museum and community sources) rather than an independent factual claim requiring new verification.

