  ('HIS-04', 'his-04-tattooing-japan-edo-visual-culture-contemporary-practice', 'history', 24, 'Tattooing in Japan, from Edo to Now', 'Tattooing in Japan from Edo Visual Culture to Contemporary Practice', 'Woodblock imagery, body-suit composition, criminalization, and the long shadow of stigma.', 'Japan taught the body-suit, and taught me patience. It also carries a stigma the West rarely sees.', '["Japan","Edo","Irezumi","Stigma"]', 'HIS-04_tattooing-japan-edo-visual-culture-contemporary-practice.md', 1120, 6, '2026-07-20', '# HIS-04 — Tattooing in Japan from Edo Visual Culture to Contemporary Practice

## 1. Abstract and Scope

This paper examines Japanese tattooing''s continuity from Edo-period woodblock (ukiyo-e) visual culture through criminalization, postwar yakuza association, and contemporary legal normalization, connecting artisan/popular-culture history, body-suit composition, tebori lineage transmission, and current debates over stigma and international practice. This paper is substantially a deepening of STY-05 (The Japanese Tattoo Continuum), which already documents this history at length; its contribution is explicitly distinguishing historical lineage (the continuous horishi/tebori apprenticeship tradition) from surface imitation (neo-Japanese and international appropriation of Japanese iconography without the underlying compositional/technical tradition), per the corpus plan''s specific instruction for this paper.

## 2. Definitions and Boundary Criteria

**Historical lineage vs. surface imitation (this paper''s organizing distinction).** This paper treats "historical lineage" as denoting direct, documented apprenticeship transmission within the horishi tradition (e.g., Horiyoshi III''s ten-year apprenticeship under Yoshitsugu Muramatsu, itself part of a chain extending back through named masters to Edo-period practice) and "surface imitation" as denoting the visual borrowing of Japanese motifs and even technique-adjacent effects (STY-05''s neo_japanese entry) without that apprenticeship transmission or the underlying compositional rule system (background field conventions, motif-pairing conventions) those motifs originally encode.

## 3. Chronology, Geography, and Transmission

This paper defers to STY-05 (Section 3) for the full documented chronology: Edo-period (1603–1868) penal tattooing and its parallel decorative transformation via Kuniyoshi''s Suikoden ukiyo-e prints; the horimono/horishi terminological self-assertion of craft status; the Meiji-era legal ban (ca. 1872–1948) and the paradoxical Yokohama-style international popularization during the same period; postwar yakuza association and enduring stigma; and the pivotal September 16, 2020 Supreme Court ruling ending tattooing''s classification as "medical practice." This paper''s specific contribution is tracing the *lineage* thread specifically: Horiyoshi III''s documented ten-year apprenticeship under Yoshitsugu Muramatsu ("Shodai Horiyoshi") represents one verifiable link in a chain of named-master transmission, and his own apprentices (Horitaka, Horitomo) extending that lineage internationally (to the United States) represents lineage''s contemporary global extension — a documented chain of transmission this paper argues is qualitatively different from, and should be modeled separately in the database from, the diffuse, non-apprenticeship-based international spread of neo-Japanese and anime/manga tattooing documented in STY-05.

## 4. Per-Entry Descriptions

This paper maps to the same catalogIds as STY-05 (irezumi, tebori_style, neo_japanese, anime), adding the lineage/imitation distinction as an explicit database-modeling recommendation: irezumi and tebori_style should be flagged as lineage-transmitted traditions with documented named-master chains; neo_japanese should be flagged as motif-borrowing without lineage transmission (already established in STY-05, Section 2); and anime should continue to be modeled as entirely outside this lineage question, per STY-05''s finding that its transmission history (Western television, internet fandom) shares no connection to horishi apprenticeship at all.

## 5. Materials, Tools, and Making Process

This paper defers to STY-05 (Section 5) and TEC-06 (Section 3, 5) for tebori''s specific tool/technique documentation, adding only that lineage transmission in the horishi tradition includes not just motif and composition knowledge but embodied technical skill (tebori''s specific hand-motion mastery, documented in TEC-06 as requiring longer to achieve than machine competency) — meaning "surface imitation" of Japanese tattoo aesthetics via machine technique, however visually accomplished, does not constitute lineage continuity even when visually similar to tebori-executed work.

## 6. Visual Grammar

This paper defers entirely to STY-05 (Section 6) for irezumi''s, tebori''s, neo-Japanese''s, and anime''s visual grammar, adding the observation that the specific compositional rule system documented for irezumi (background wind/wave/cloud fields, motif-pairing conventions, full-body-suit planning) is itself part of what lineage transmission carries forward, while neo-Japanese''s explicit departure from these rules (STY-05, Section 2) is precisely what marks it as surface imitation of iconography rather than continuation of the compositional tradition — a point this paper treats as the clearest concrete illustration of this paper''s central lineage/imitation distinction.

## 7. Skin, Anatomy, Placement, Healing, Aging, and Technical Limitations

This paper defers to STY-05 (Section 7) for full-suit scale, concealment-driven placement logic, and tebori-versus-machine healing considerations, adding no new material.

## 8. Social Meaning, Identity, Ritual, Memory, and Client Narrative

This paper defers to STY-05 (Section 8) for irezumi''s endurance/loyalty/reclaimed-shame social meaning and tebori''s gaman-linked pain-as-meaning framing, adding the observation that this social meaning is itself lineage-dependent: a wearer receiving irezumi from a horishi within the documented apprenticeship chain is participating in a social meaning system extending back through named masters to Edo-period practice, while a wearer receiving neo-Japanese or non-lineage machine-executed "Japanese-style" work, however beautiful, is not participating in that same continuous social-meaning chain — a distinction this paper argues clients and the database''s AI-support layer should both be able to articulate honestly rather than blurring.

## 9. Cultural Ownership, Sacred/Restricted Elements, and Ethical Use

This paper defers to STY-05 (Section 9) for the full ethical treatment already established there (irezumi/tebori as a living national tradition with an active internal authenticity debate rather than a formally restricted practice; neo-Japanese''s labeling-accuracy consideration; anime''s IP/copyright consideration rather than cultural-sensitivity consideration). This paper''s contribution is sharpening the specific recommendation that the database''s sensitivityFlag and history fields for irezumi and tebori_style explicitly note the lineage-transmission chain (naming Horiyoshi III and his documented teacher/apprentice relationships as one concrete, verifiable example) rather than treating "respect the tradition" as a generic, undifferentiated instruction.

## 10. Disputes, Regional Variation, Source Limitations, and Confidence Level

**No new disputes were identified beyond those already documented in STY-05.** This paper''s lineage/imitation distinction is this paper''s own analytical framing rather than a claim requiring independent new sourcing, built directly on STY-05''s already-verified biographical chain (Horiyoshi III → Muramatsu; Horiyoshi III → Horitaka/Horitomo) and compositional-rule documentation. Confidence: high, as a conservative extension of STY-05''s existing sourcing.

