  ('HIS-08', 'his-08-revival-platform-culture-globalization-conventions-social-media', 'history', 28, 'From Revival to Platform Culture', 'From Revival to Platform Culture: Globalization, Conventions, Social Media, and Hybrid Styles', 'Conventions, artist mobility, and social media — how microstyles are named, copied, and spread overnight.', 'The feed names a new style each month. Speed is exciting, but authorship and origin still deserve our care.', '["Revival","Conventions","Social Media","Authorship"]', 'HIS-08_revival-platform-culture-globalization-conventions-social-media.md', 1659, 8, '2026-07-20', '# HIS-08 — From Revival to Platform Culture: Globalization, Conventions, Social Media, and Hybrid Styles

## 1. Abstract and Scope

This paper documents late-20th- and 21st-century globalization, international convention culture, artist mobility, photography, social platforms, rapid naming of microstyles, copying, authorship, and cultural appropriation — tracing tattooing''s transmission infrastructure from the first physical tattoo conventions (1976) through the platform-driven, algorithmically accelerated trend cycle documented across this corpus''s more recent style entries (STY-06, STY-07, STY-08). This paper contributes newly researched material on tattoo convention history, a physical/institutional globalization infrastructure predating and then coexisting with the internet-era acceleration this corpus has documented extensively elsewhere.

## 2. Definitions and Boundary Criteria

**Convention culture vs. platform culture (this paper''s organizing distinction).** This paper treats "convention culture" as denoting the physical, in-person, artist-to-artist and artist-to-public gathering infrastructure that began in 1976 and continues today, and "platform culture" as denoting the internet/social-media-based transmission infrastructure (Instagram, TikTok) documented extensively across STY-06 through STY-08 as driving the 2010s–2020s acceleration of style naming, trend cycling, and client-to-artist discovery. This paper treats these as sequential but coexisting infrastructures rather than one having simply replaced the other — contemporary convention culture, per this paper''s newly researched sourcing, remains active and growing (the Houston World Tattoo Convention''s 50th anniversary event, per sources reviewed, was planned for April 2026) even as platform culture has become the dominant discovery/trend mechanism this corpus''s more recent style papers document.

## 3. Chronology, Geography, and Transmission

**The first World Tattoo Convention (Houston, January 24–25, 1976) — newly researched for this paper.** Lyle Tuttle and Dave Yurkew hosted what Wikipedia and multiple independently corroborating trade sources document as the first recorded tattoo convention in history, in Houston, Texas, drawing roughly 130 tattoo artists — including figures already documented in this corpus''s tattoo-renaissance material (STY-01, Section 3): Don Ed Hardy attended, alongside Shanghai Kate, Sailor West, Mr. Tramp, Charlie Potter, and Crazy Ace. Tuttle himself is quoted describing it as "the event that changed tattooing forever." Yurkew, then president of the North American Tattoo Club, went on to host six consecutive World Tattoo Conventions through 1982. This paper connects this directly to STY-01''s already-documented "tattoo renaissance" (Arnold Rubin''s term, STY-01 Section 3): the convention functioned as a physical infrastructure making the renaissance''s cross-pollination (Hardy''s documented 1977 Las Vegas convention meeting with Jack Rudy and Charlie Cartwright, per STY-01''s Chicano-crossover documentation) possible at scale for the first time, formalizing what had previously been a smaller, more secretive trade network.

**Subsequent convention infrastructure expansion (1977–present).** The International Tattoo Artists Association held its first convention in 1977 (Reno); the first National Convention followed in 1979 (Denver), with international representation already present (German artists Horst Streckenbach and Manfred Kohrs among the documented 1979 speaker list). This paper''s sourcing documents contemporary convention culture as a large, continuing global industry — citing, for example, a 2025 Villain Arts-organized traveling tattoo festival''s first stop in San Antonio expecting up to 30,000 attendees — indicating that convention culture has scaled dramatically rather than being superseded by platform culture, even as its function has shifted somewhat from Tuttle/Yurkew''s original artist-community-building purpose toward a more commercialized festival/entertainment model incorporating sideshow acts, celebrity appearances, and vendor markets alongside tattooing itself.

**Platform culture''s acceleration (2010s–present).** This paper cross-references, rather than re-documents, this corpus''s extensive existing platform-culture documentation: the Bang Bang/JonBoy fine-line boom''s explicit Instagram dependency (STY-08, Section 3); Sticker/Patchwork''s TikTok/Instagram-driven 2020s emergence as a diffuse trend without a credited founder (STY-07, Section 3); Sketch style''s social-media-driven popularity arc (STY-06, Section 3); and Cyber Sigilism''s rapid 2020s rise, described in this corpus''s foundational document as among the most-searched tattoo styles by 2025 (STY-07, Section 3). This paper treats the shift from convention-mediated to platform-mediated style transmission as the central structural change in globalization infrastructure across the roughly fifty-year span this paper covers, with the specific transition point falling somewhere in the mid-to-late 2000s (coinciding with STY-08''s documented Bang Bang/JonBoy-era Instagram adoption) rather than at any single sharply defined date.

## 4. Per-Entry Descriptions

This paper does not map to specific style/technique catalogIds but provides infrastructure-level historical framing applicable across this corpus''s more recent, platform-culture-associated entries (STY-06, STY-07, STY-08 in particular).

## 5. Materials, Tools, and Making Process

Not directly applicable to this infrastructure/transmission-focused paper; see the relevant style/technique papers for material and technical documentation.

## 6. Visual Grammar

**Rapid microstyle naming as a platform-culture-specific phenomenon.** This paper observes, building on findings already established across STY-06 and STY-07, that platform culture has enabled a qualitatively faster style-naming cycle than convention culture''s slower, artist-network-mediated diffusion allowed: styles like Sticker/Patchwork, Cyber Sigilism, and Sketch acquired names and reached broad public recognition within a few years of emergence (STY-07, STY-06), a pace this paper contrasts with the decades-long diffusion documented for, e.g., Neo-Traditional and New School''s 1970s–90s emergence and gradual naming consolidation (STY-01, Section 10).

## 7. Skin, Anatomy, Placement, Healing, Aging, and Technical Limitations

Not directly applicable to this infrastructure-focused paper.

## 8. Social Meaning, Identity, Ritual, Memory, and Client Narrative

**Convention culture''s documented "brotherhood" and community-building function.** This paper''s newly researched sourcing includes a direct practitioner account (a San Francisco tattoo artist quoted at the first International London Tattoo Convention) describing conventions'' social function explicitly in terms of professional community and friendship: "you make friends from all over the world, so it''s like a big reunion every time there''s a convention" — a social function this paper treats as historically prior to, and structurally different from, platform culture''s primarily client-discovery/trend-visibility function documented across STY-06–STY-08, even though both infrastructures serve globalization broadly.

**Copying, authorship, and appropriation as concerns intensified (not created) by platform culture.** This paper notes that authorship and copying concerns predate platform culture — this corpus''s STY-01 documentation of Lew Alberts'' and Bert Grimm''s disputed flash-origination narrative (Section 10) is a pre-internet-era authorship dispute — but argues platform culture has intensified these concerns by making both direct visual copying (screenshotting and replicating another artist''s design) and rapid, wide-scale attribution-free trend diffusion (as documented for Sticker/Patchwork and Cyber Sigilism, both credited to no single named originator) far easier and faster than convention-era in-person artist networks allowed.

## 9. Cultural Ownership, Sacred/Restricted Elements, and Ethical Use

**Platform culture''s specific role in accelerating appropriation risk, cross-referenced from prior papers.** This paper connects its infrastructure-level analysis to the appropriation concerns already documented at length across STY-03 (Section 9, blackwork/tribal), STY-04 (Section 9, Pacific traditions), and STY-07 (Section 9, cyber sigilism''s inherited neo-tribal appropriation debate): platform culture''s rapid, decontextualized image circulation (a design screenshot stripped of its original cultural or technical context, reshared and reinterpreted at scale) structurally amplifies the appropriation risk these prior papers document, since platform-native trend diffusion typically carries far less attribution or context than convention-era artist-to-artist transmission, where at least some direct conversation about a design''s origin and meaning was more structurally likely to occur.

## 10. Disputes, Regional Variation, Source Limitations, and Confidence Level

**The precise "platform culture supersedes convention culture" narrative should be resisted.** This paper explicitly does not claim platform culture has replaced convention culture, given its newly researched finding that convention attendance has scaled to tens of thousands of attendees at individual contemporary events; this paper treats the two infrastructures as coexisting and serving partly distinct functions (professional community/skill transmission for conventions; public trend discovery/naming for platforms) rather than one succeeding the other.

**Source limitations.** Convention history material is newly researched and drawn primarily from Wikipedia (itself citing named sources) and multiple independently corroborating trade-blog sources describing the same 1976 Houston event consistently; contemporary convention attendance figures (e.g., the cited 30,000-attendee San Antonio estimate) derive from event-promotional sourcing and should be treated as organizer-reported rather than independently audited figures.

